```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal;
.title "Journal-Style Output";
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
  var make msrp;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
In this example, the `ods pdf` statement specifies the output file name "output.pdf" and the style options for the journal format. The `title` statement sets the title of the output.

The `proc print` statement prints the SAS data set `sashelp.cars` with columns `make` and `msrp`. In the journal format, the output will be formatted to resemble a printed journal.

The `ods pdf close` statement closes the PDF file.

You can customize the journal style by specifying additional options in the `ods` statement. For example, you can adjust the font, font size, and spacing using the `ods` macro variable `font`, `font_size`, and `line_height`. Here's an example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" style=journal font=fallback:v Times-Roman font_size=12 line_height=1.5;
.title "Journal-Style Output";
proc print data=sashelp.cars;
  var make msrp;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
This code sets the font to Times Roman, font size to 12 points, and line height to 1.5 times the default value.

